-compliant, or commercially absurd claims pass into a polished PDF.\n\n---\n\n1. **(severity HIGH) — Cost stack / brief-vs-design mismatch**  \n   **Finding:** The design violates the brief’s £1,800 maximum OEM price before any channel or installation markup.  \n   **Evidence:** “= OEM transfer price £1,912.22” and “Manufactured at a target OEM unit cost of £1,800”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 5 cost stack — `src/chain/stages/cost/costStack.ts` does not enforce hard brief constraints after BoM aggregation.  \n   **Fix:** Add a post-cost hard gate: `assert(oem_transfer_price <= brief.max_oem_price)`; if exceeded, emit FAIL, not a report headline.\n\n2. **(severity HIGH) — Cost realism / market sanity**  \n   **Finding:** The installed ASP is implausibly low for an 8 kW UK MCS-installed ASHP system and is self-flagged as below market.  \n   **Evidence:** “£368 per kW thermal installed — 39% below typical installed-ASP range (band £600–£900)”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 5 cost model — `src/chain/stages/cost/costStack.ts` uses a flat 40% installation factor instead of UK ASHP fixed-cost installation model.  \n   **Fix:** Replace percentage installation markup with class-specific install model: base labour + electrical + plumbing + commissioning + MCS paperwork + margin; reject outputs outside market prior unless justified.\n\n3. **(severity HIGH) — Cost reference contamination**  \n   **Finding:** The 8 kW residential report is using a 30 kW monobloc cost benchmark, contaminating the cost sanity check.  \n   **Evidence:** “Installed ASP for 30 kW R290 monobloc: hardware (~£9-13k) + scaled pipework + electrics + MCS commissioning = £18-27k total = £600-900/kW.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 5 reference retrieval — `src/chain/stages/cost/referenceAnchors.ts` retrieves nearest heat-pump corpus entries without capacity-band filtering.  \n   **Fix:** Add capacity-class filter before reference-anchor selection: residential `<12 kW`, small commercial `12–30 kW`, commercial `>30 kW`; never compare 8 kW domestic to 30 kW light-commercial.\n\n4. **(severity HIGH) — Physical envelope impossible**  \n   **Finding:** The stated product envelope height is 144 mm, which cannot contain the compressor, fan, coil, casing, or 200 L cylinder.  \n   **Evidence:** “Envelope footprint 1200 × 800 × 144 mm (L × W × H)”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 derived physical spec — `src/chain/stages/derivePhysicalSpec.ts` confuses pallet deck height with product height.  \n   **Fix:** Split logistics envelope from product dimensions; validate product bounding box against largest included component dimensions before rendering.\n\n5. **(severity HIGH) — Internal dimensional contradiction**  \n   **Finding:** The report says the product envelope is 144 mm high while the outdoor casing alone is 850 mm high.  \n   **Evidence:** “Envelope footprint 1200 × 800 × 144 mm (L × W × H)” and “950x400x850 mm assembled”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 module geometry composer — `src/chain/stages/modules/geometryConsistency.ts` is missing max-component-dimension validation.  \n   **Fix:** Add `envelope.H >= max(component.H)` check across all modules and fail report generation on violation.\n\n6. **(severity HIGH) — Indoor cylinder wrongly included in outdoor product envelope / BoM**  \n   **Finding:** The report mixes an outdoor ASHP unit and a 200 L indoor DHW cylinder as if they are one bounded product.  \n   **Evidence:** “Deployment envelope Euro Pallet (EPAL-1)” and “The DHW cylinder consists of a 200 L stainless steel tank”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 architecture decomposition — `src/chain/stages/decomposeProduct.ts` does not distinguish base appliance scope from optional system accessories.  \n   **Fix:** Add `product_scope` schema: `outdoor_unit`, `indoor_hydraulic_module`, `DHW_cylinder_optional`, `installation_kit`; cost and envelope must be computed per scope.\n\n7. **(severity HIGH) — BS EN 14511 rated performance not actually specified**  \n   **Finding:** The brief explicitly requires A7/W35 rated conditions, but the report never gives rated COP, water flow rate, leaving-water temperature tolerance, or test condition table.  \n   **Evidence:** “Converts electrical energy to mechanical work and transfers heat to deliver 8 kW of rated thermal output at A7/W35 conditions.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 performance synthesis — `src/chain/stages/performance/heatPumpRatings.ts` treats a prose mention of A7/W35 as satisfying BS EN 14511.  \n   **Fix:** Add mandatory `rated_conditions` object for heat pumps: `A7/W35 capacity`, `COP`, `flow_temp`, `return_temp`, `water_flow_lpm`, `ambient_db/wb`, `standard`.\n\n8. **(severity HIGH) — SCOP misused as rated/full-load COP**  \n   **Finding:** The report derives 2.28 kW electrical input from 8 kW / SCOP 3.5, which is invalid because SCOP is seasonal part-load performance, not rated A7/W35 COP.  \n   **Evidence:** “drawing up to 2.28 kW of electrical power to achieve a SCOP of 3.5”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 thermodynamic derivation — `src/chain/stages/performance/derivePower.ts` uses `P_elec = Q_nominal / SCOP`.  \n   **Fix:** Use `rated_COP_A7W35` for rated electrical input and reserve SCOP for BS EN 14825 seasonal bin calculation.\n\n9. **(severity HIGH) — Impossible capacity claim down to -25 °C**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims the system maintains 8 kW output down to -25 °C without backup or derating, which is physically unrealistic for this class and cost.  \n   **Evidence:** “To maintain the 8 kW output during freezing UK winters down to -25 °C”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 prose generator — `src/chain/stages/modules/generateModuleNarrative.ts` copies operating envelope into capacity guarantee.  \n   **Fix:** Add validator requiring capacity curve points: `A7/W35`, `A2/W35`, `A-7/W35`, `A-15/W35`; prohibit “maintain rated output across envelope” unless compressor/heat-exchanger sizing supports it.\n\n10. **(severity HIGH) — Evaporator rating contradicts low-temperature output claim**  \n   **Finding:** The evaporator is only specified as 8 kW at A7, not at -25 °C, so the report’s low-temperature capacity claim is unsupported.  \n   **Evidence:** “rated 8 kW at A7, 5 °C superheat”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 component sizing — `src/chain/stages/sizing/heatExchangers.ts` accepts a single nominal point and lets it support all ambient conditions.  \n   **Fix:** Require heat exchanger capacity map or derating model over ambient bins before allowing minimum-ambient output claims.\n\n11. **(severity HIGH) — R410A compressor selected for R290 system**  \n   **Finding:** The selected Copeland ZP25K5E compressor is not a safe R290 compressor selection.  \n   **Evidence:** “The Copeland ZP25K5E-TFM is an R410A scroll compressor, not rated or safe for R290 (propane) refrigerant.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 supplier/part selection — `src/chain/stages/bom/selectParts.ts` matches compressor by capacity only, not refrigerant compatibility and A3 safety approval.  \n   **Fix:** Add hard filters for compressor: `refrigerant_approved.includes(\"R290\")`, `A3_ignition_source_assessment`, `application = heat_pump`, and reject R410A/R32-only models.\n\n12. **(severity HIGH) — Physics critic flags fatal issue but report still presents design as valid**  \n   **Finding:** The pipeline detected a severe compressor safety failure but allowed the main report to remain procurement-like.  \n   **Evidence:** “Physics critic flagged 3 high-severity engineering issues (4/10 engineering-plausibility score).”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 6 report gating — `src/chain/stages/review/physicsCriticGate.ts` emits manual-review notes but does not fail high-severity safety findings.  \n   **Fix:** Promote high-confidence/high-severity physics critic findings to blocking errors for product classes involving mains, pressure, flammables, or rotating machinery.\n\n13. **(severity HIGH) — EMI filter undersized for compressor/inverter input current**  \n   **Finding:** The specified 6 A EMI filter cannot support the stated compressor electrical load.  \n   **Evidence:** “The Schaffner FN2030-6-06 EMI filter is rated for 6 A. However, the 2.5 kW electrical compressor draws approximately 10.87 A continuous at 230 V AC”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 BoM selection — `src/chain/stages/bom/electricalSizing.ts` does not cross-check current ratings against load.  \n   **Fix:** Add electrical load propagation and require every series component current rating ≥ continuous load × derating factor.\n\n14. **(severity HIGH) — DC link capacitance absurdly undersized**  \n   **Finding:** Two 10 µF film capacitors are not a credible DC link for a 2.5 kW single-phase inverter drive.  \n   **Evidence:** “The DC link uses two 10 µF capacitors (EPCOS B32778E4106K000) for a total of 20 µF.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 component selection — `src/chain/stages/bom/inverterSizing.ts` selects capacitors by voltage only, not ripple, energy storage, or topology.  \n   **Fix:** Implement inverter sizing equations for DC-link ripple current, capacitance, bus voltage, hold-up, and thermal life; block if selected capacitor bank fails.\n\n15. **(severity HIGH) — Pressure relief and HP switch sequencing is unsafe**  \n   **Finding:** The high-pressure switch is set above the relief valve, so the system vents before the electrical safety trip.  \n   **Evidence:** “40 bar pressure relief valve and a high-pressure safety switch” and “42 bar normally-closed high-pressure safety switch”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 safety logic — `src/chain/stages/safety/pressureProtection.ts` has no ordering rule for trip thresholds.  \n   **Fix:** Add rule: `HP_cutout < PRV_set_pressure < PS_design`; enforce margins by refrigerant and component PS.\n\n16. **(severity HIGH) — K10 reference graph says R290 HP switch should be much lower than emitted design**  \n   **Finding:** The design emits a 42 bar R290 HP switch while the reference graph expects a 26 bar R290 hardwired trip.  \n   **Evidence:** “HP switch (31.5 bar R32 / 26 bar R290) and LP switch hard-wired to compressor contactor coil — trips”  \n   **Root cause:** K10 graph check is shadow-only — `src/registries/k10/heat-pump-residential.ts` is diagnostic and not enforced.  \n   **Fix:** Promote K10 required edges and threshold checks to enforcing mode for heat-pump class before PDF generation.\n\n17. **(severity HIGH) — R290 charge compliance impossible because refrigerant charge is never stated**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims compliance with A3 refrigerant charge limits without specifying refrigerant charge mass.  \n   **Evidence:** “The design strictly adheres to IEC 60335-2-40 Annex GG charge limits”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 safety/compliance synthesis — `src/chain/stages/compliance/flammableRefrigerants.ts` allows standard-name assertions without input variables.  \n   **Fix:** Require `refrigerant_charge_g`, installation location, room-volume exposure, refrigerant circuit type, and Annex GG calculation output before compliance claim.\n\n18. **(severity HIGH) — Leak detector range statement is nonsensical**  \n   **Finding:** A sensor range of “0 to 1% of LEL” is unusably tiny and likely confused with 0–100% LEL or 0–1% volume.  \n   **Evidence:** “continuously monitors for R290 concentrations from 0 to 1% of the lower explosive limit.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 sensor generation — `src/chain/stages/modules/sensorNarrative.ts` lacks units normalization for gas detection.  \n   **Fix:** Add gas-sensor schema requiring `range_ppm`, `%LEL`, alarm threshold, calibration gas, response time, and operating environment.\n\n19. **(severity HIGH) — Propane sensor operating temperature does not meet product ambient envelope**  \n   **Finding:** The selected gas sensor is rated only to -10 °C while the product claims -25 °C operation.  \n   **Evidence:** “operating over -10 to +60 °C” and “Operating temperature -25 to 45 °C”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 environmental compatibility — `src/chain/stages/bom/environmentalFit.ts` does not compare component temperature range to product envelope.  \n   **Fix:** Add universal check: every safety-critical component must cover full product operating range plus derating margin.\n\n20. **(severity HIGH) — Claimed certifications for commodity sensors/relays are hallucinated**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims IEC 60335-2-40 Annex GG certification for a Figaro gas sensor and IEC/BS EN certification for a generic relay in a way that is not credible.  \n   **Evidence:** “certified to IEC 60335-2-40 Annex GG” and “certified to IEC 60335-2-40, BS EN 378-1”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 certification filler — `src/chain/stages/bom/certificationTagger.ts` attaches product-level standards to components without datasheet evidence.  \n   **Fix:** Separate `component_certifications` from `system_applicable_standards`; only attach component certs verified from datasheets.\n\n21. **(severity HIGH) — MCS 020 noise requirement is misrepresented as a 56 dBA sound-power target**  \n   **Finding:** MCS 020 is not simply “outdoor unit sound power below 56 dBA”; it is a site-specific neighbour noise calculation.  \n   **Evidence:** “To comply with MCS 020 planning requirements, the outdoor unit features extensive acoustic damping to maintain sound power below 56 dBA.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 standards expansion — `src/registries/standards/heat-pump-residential.ts` collapses MCS 020 calculator into a single generic sound-power number.  \n   **Fix:** Encode MCS 020 as calculator inputs: declared sound power to BS EN 12102-1, distance, directivity, barriers, reflective surfaces, background correction, nearest habitable-room window.\n\n22. **(severity HIGH) — MCS 020 table contains corrupted threshold text**  \n   **Finding:** The report renders the MCS noise limit as “d42 dBA,” showing a broken comparator/entity.  \n   **Evidence:** “UK planning-permission permitted-development limit for noise: d42 dBA at 1 m from neighbour.”  \n   **Root cause:** Report renderer / text sanitizer — `src/report/renderComplianceTable.ts` corrupts `≤ 42 dB(A)` into `d42 dBA`.  \n   **Fix:** Add unit/equality glyph tests and render standards thresholds as structured fields, not free text.\n\n23. **(severity HIGH) — UK regulatory scope is wrong / incomplete**  \n   **Finding:** The report targets the UK but relies heavily on EU directives instead of UK statutory instruments for UKCA.  \n   **Evidence:** “Heat pump compliance is bounded by refrigerant regulation (EU 517/2014 F-gas - drives R290 adoption), pressure-equipment safety (PED 2014/68), and seasonal performance reporting (EN 14825).”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 compliance registry — `src/registries/standards/jurisdictions.ts` does not map EU directives to UK post-Brexit equivalents.  \n   **Fix:** Add UKCA mapping layer: EESR 2016, EMC Regulations 2016, Supply of Machinery Safety Regulations 2008, Pressure Equipment Safety Regulations 2016, UK Ecodesign/Energy Labelling, UK F-gas.\n\n24. **(severity MED) — Outdated F-gas regulation reference**  \n   **Finding:** The report cites EU 517/2014 as the governing F-gas regulation, but EU F-gas has been recast and UK has its own retained/amended regime.  \n   **Evidence:** “EU 517/2014 Regulation on fluorinated greenhouse gases (F-gas)”  \n   **Root cause:** Standards registry stale data — `src/registries/standards/eu-fgas.ts` not versioned by date/jurisdiction.  \n   **Fix:** Add regulation versioning with effective dates; for UK reports, cite UK F-gas regime separately from EU 2024/573.\n\n25. **(severity HIGH) — Compliance gate warns but does not fail missing mandatory UK electrical safety requirement**  \n   **Finding:** The report knows EESR 2016 is missing but still presents a UK market design.  \n   **Evidence:** “Compliance gate WARN: 1 mandatory standard not declared in the brief for the detected jurisdictions (UK, EU).”  \n   **Root cause:** G1b compliance gate — `src/chain/stages/compliance/g1bGate.ts` treats missing mandatory standards as WARN instead of FAIL.  \n   **Fix:** Change mandatory missing jurisdictional standards from warning to blocking failure for regulated hardware.\n\n26. **(severity HIGH) — BoM pricing is commercially impossible for major assemblies**  \n   **Finding:** Critical assemblies are priced at toy-level costs: compressor £17, condenser £2.96, evaporator £2.96, inverter PCB £1.14, HMI £0.14.  \n   **Evidence:** “Bldc Compressor Motor Copeland (Emerson) ZP25K5E-TFM ~ £17.08” and “Brazed Plate Condenser SWEP (Dover) B10T-30 ~ £2.96”  \n   **Root cause:** Engine B pricing — `src/chain/stages/bom/priceParts.ts` applies generic class curves and fallback web estimates without assembly-level floors.  \n   **Fix:** Add class-specific minimum credible costs and supplier-quote requirements for compressors, heat exchangers, cylinders, electronics, pumps, valves, and HMI.\n\n27. **(severity HIGH) — 5,000 units/year brief ignored in cost curves**  \n   **Finding:** The pricing engine uses 100,000 annual volume curves while the brief says 5,000 units/year.  \n   **Evidence:** “5,000 units annually” and “annual volume=100,000”  \n   **Root cause:** Engine B batch economics — `src/chain/stages/bom/classCostCurves.ts` uses default annual volume instead of `brief.production_volume`.  \n   **Fix:** Thread `brief.volume_per_year` into all pricing curves and add a report assertion showing the volume used for every scale factor.\n\n28. **(severity HIGH) — Raw BoM scale factor unjustifiably suppresses costs**  \n   **Finding:** A blanket 0.325 scale factor is applied despite only four live quotes and many fabricated/unverified parts.  \n   **Evidence:** “Raw BoM scale factor 0.325 (per-class batch economics)” and “4 actual ~ 140 estimate”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 pricing — `src/chain/stages/bom/scaleDistributorPrices.ts` applies scale discount even to unverified estimates.  \n   **Fix:** Only apply volume scaling to verified distributor/OEM quotes with MOQ/volume evidence; otherwise mark as unscaled estimate or TBD.\n\n29. **(severity HIGH) — “0 TBD” contradicts extensive unverified/fake-part status**  \n   **Finding:** The BoM claims zero TBD lines while 94 part lines are unverified.  \n   **Evidence:** “0 TBD” and “94 part lines are unverified — see Appendix A for the audit trail.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4.5 verification integration — `src/chain/stages/procurement/verifyPartNumbers.ts` does not propagate verification failure into BoM line status.  \n   **Fix:** Add `verification_status` to BoM schema and force `TBD` or `UNVERIFIED` costing state for fabricated/uncertain parts.\n\n30. **(severity HIGH) — Appendix says fabricated SKUs were stripped but BoM still contains and prices them**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims fake SKUs were removed, yet the BoM still lists them with prices.  \n   **Evidence:** “Stripped (fakes): 60” and “The items below could not be confidently verified”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4.5 part verification — `src/chain/stages/procurement/stripFabricatedParts.ts` updates appendix data but not canonical BoM data used by renderer.  \n   **Fix:** Make verified BoM the single source of truth; renderer must consume post-verification BoM, not pre-verification BoM.\n\n31. **(severity HIGH) — Procurement readiness is falsely implied despite only four live distributor quotes**  \n   **Finding:** A 144-line BoM with only four actual quotes is presented with a precise grand total.  \n   **Evidence:** “144 part lines · 12 modules” and “4 actual ~ 140 estimate”  \n   **Root cause:** Report renderer — `src/report/renderBomSummary.ts` overstates precision by summing weak estimates into a currency-exact total.  \n   **Fix:** Render estimate ranges and confidence intervals; require minimum quote coverage threshold before showing a single-point BoM total.\n\n32. **(severity MED) — Duplicate BoM extraction creates repeated parts**  \n   **Finding:** The defrost relay appears twice even though the sub-module description only calls for one relay.  \n   **Evidence:** “Defrost Relay Omron G2R-1-E £2.42” and again “Defrost Relay Omron G2R-1-E £2.42”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 BoM extraction — `src/chain/stages/bom/extractBomFromWords.ts` double-counts repeated noun phrases across narrative and structured submodule word list.  \n   **Fix:** Deduplicate by `(module, submodule, normalized_part_role, manufacturer, part_number)` unless explicit quantity is present.\n\n33. **(severity MED) — Component cardinality hard-code broken**  \n   **Finding:** Several submodules say “5 components” while listing more than five parts.  \n   **Evidence:** “The internal harness distributes 5 components: Lapp Group high-voltage DC bus cable, Alpha Wire sensor wiring loom, Lapp Group cable gland set, TDK EPCOS ferrite ring core, HellermannTyton spiral wrap tubing, Bossard grounding stud, Bossard grounding washer, and Phoenix Contact EMC shield clamp”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 submodule prose — `src/chain/stages/modules/submoduleNarrative.ts` hard-codes “5 components” from prompt template.  \n   **Fix:** Generate count from parsed component array length; add renderer test that declared count equals listed count.\n\n34. **(severity HIGH) — Major assemblies are split into impossible subparts causing double counting and undercounting**  \n   **Finding:** The compressor is split into “BLDC compressor motor,” “compressor scroll set,” and “scroll compressor mechanism” instead of one certified hermetic compressor assembly.  \n   **Evidence:** “Bldc Compressor Motor Copeland (Emerson) ZP25K5E-TFM” and “Scroll Compressor Mechanism Copeland MECH-SCROLL-25”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 module decomposition — `src/chain/stages/decomposeProduct.ts` decomposes sealed OEM assemblies into non-procurable internal mechanisms.  \n   **Fix:** Add non-decomposable assembly registry for hermetic compressors, pumps, certified pressure components, and inverter modules unless designing from first principles.\n\n35. **(severity HIGH) — Fan selection is dimensionally incoherent**  \n   **Finding:** The design describes a 500 mm fan but the BoM uses an ebm-papst A2E200-class part, which is a ~200 mm fan family.  \n   **Evidence:** “It includes a 500 mm composite axial fan blade” and “Axial Fan Blade ebm-papst A2E200-AK-02”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4 part selection — `src/chain/stages/bom/selectMechanicalParts.ts` does not validate dimensional attributes against selected SKU.  \n   **Fix:** Require dimensional matching for fans: diameter, airflow, static pressure, speed, noise, motor type; reject SKU if diameter mismatch >5%.\n\n36. **(severity HIGH) — DHW cylinder safety package incomplete for UK unvented cylinder installation**  \n   **Finding:** The report includes a 200 L unvented DHW cylinder but omits the required full G3 safety/control package such as inlet control group, expansion control, tundish, discharge pipework, and thermostatic controls.  \n   **Evidence:** “A Reliance Water Controls temperature and pressure relief valve … certified to WRAS, BS EN 1490, Building Regs G3.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 standards expansion / module completeness — `src/registries/classes/heat-pump-residential.ts` treats one T&P valve as satisfying G3 cylinder requirements.  \n   **Fix:** Add DHW-cylinder subsystem template with mandatory G3 components and installation notes; distinguish potable expansion vessel from hydronic expansion vessel.\n\n37. **(severity HIGH) — Hydronic expansion vessel confused with DHW cylinder expansion needs**  \n   **Finding:** The report adds an 8 L hydronic expansion vessel but does not provide potable DHW expansion control for the 200 L cylinder.  \n   **Evidence:** “The hydronic loop incorporates a Zilmet CAL-PRO 8-litre expansion vessel assembly to manage thermal volumetric expansion of the heating water”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 hydraulic model — `src/chain/stages/sizing/hydronics.ts` has one generic “expansion vessel” slot and no separation between sealed primary circuit and potable secondary circuit.  \n   **Fix:** Model separate circuits: refrigerant, primary hydronic, potable DHW; each must have pressure protection, expansion, relief, isolation, and compliance components.\n\n38. **(severity MED) — Filling loop WRAS claim is oversimplified**  \n   **Finding:** The report implies a single filling loop valve/check diaphragm ensures WRAS compliance, which is not enough for UK backflow/cross-contamination requirements.  \n   **Evidence:** “ensuring strict WRAS compliance and preventing cross-contamination between the heating circuit and the potable mains supply.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 hydronic prose — `src/chain/stages/modules/hydronicNarrative.ts` converts component presence into compliance assertion.  \n   **Fix:** Add UK water-regs backflow schema requiring appropriate double check valve, detachable loop/procedure, category assessment, and installation notes.\n\n39. **(severity HIGH) — Refrigerant PRV discharge is not safely engineered for propane**  \n   **Finding:** The report specifies a PRV discharge tube but gives no safe discharge routing, vent location, ignition exclusion, or sizing for R290.  \n   **Evidence:** “a copper PRV discharge tube”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 safety protection — `src/chain/stages/safety/refrigerantRelief.ts` models PRV as a component, not a discharge system.  \n   **Fix:** Add mandatory PRV discharge design fields for A3 refrigerants: relief capacity, pipe sizing, outlet location, dispersion, ignition-source exclusion, weather protection.\n\n40. **(severity MED) — Thermal management submodule is nonsensical for a hermetic compressor**  \n   **Finding:** A compressor cold plate and coolant manifold are added without a defined coolant loop or need, increasing complexity and leak risk.  \n   **Evidence:** “a 180x120x12 mm compressor cold plate (in Aluminium 6063-T5), a CW617N brass coolant manifold”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 module expansion — `src/chain/stages/modules/expandSafetyProtection.ts` hallucinated generic electronics cooling architecture into a heat-pump compressor context.  \n   **Fix:** Add class-specific allowed submodules; compressor thermal management should be crankcase heater, discharge temp sensing, envelope control, and airflow, not arbitrary cold plates.\n\n41. **(severity MED) — Defrost architecture confused between basepan heating and evaporator heating**  \n   **Finding:** The design has a 150 W basepan heater and also two 75 W defrost heater elements, duplicating or confusing defrost functions.  \n   **Evidence:** “150 W silicone-sheathed basepan heater cable” and “two 75 W Incoloy 800-sheathed defrost heater elements”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 module merge — `src/chain/stages/modules/mergeSubmodules.ts` lets environmental-interface and safety-protection both create defrost hardware.  \n   **Fix:** Add functional ownership map: defrost belongs to refrigerant cycle/control plus basepan drain heating; deduplicate by function before BoM extraction.\n\n42. **(severity HIGH) — “Active defrost” needs reversing-valve/control validation, not just a heater cable**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims active defrost and continuous low-temperature output but does not size defrost energy, cycle penalty, condensate management, or SCOP impact.  \n   **Evidence:** “a four-way reversing valve to enable active defrost cycles”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 thermodynamic model — `src/chain/stages/performance/defrostModel.ts` missing or not invoked.  \n   **Fix:** Add defrost model with frost conditions, reverse-cycle duration, crankcase/sump limits, fan stop/start, basepan heating, water-side temperature dip, and SCOP derate.\n\n43. **(severity MED) — Risk section contains wrong pressure-switch location**  \n   **Finding:** The FMEA says the high-pressure cutout is on the suction line, which is technically wrong.  \n   **Evidence:** “high-pressure cutout switch on suction line”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 3 risk-library text — `src/registries/risks/heatPumpHazards.ts` contains incorrect canned mitigation prose.  \n   **Fix:** Correct library entry to “high-pressure cutout on discharge/high side” and add unit tests for common refrigeration safety topology.\n\n44. **(severity MED) — R290 service/decommissioning references F-gas certification incorrectly**  \n   **Finding:** The FMEA frames R290 service recovery through F-gas certification even though propane is not an F-gas; competence and flammable refrigerant handling still matter, but the regulatory framing is wrong.  \n   **Evidence:** “F-gas-certified installer requirement (UK MCS or EU 303/2008 cert)”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 3 risk registry — `src/registries/risks/refrigerantService.ts` uses HFC boilerplate for natural refrigerants.  \n   **Fix:** Split refrigerant service rules by refrigerant class: HFC/HFO F-gas obligations vs hydrocarbon A3 competence, DSEAR/ignition controls, recovery/venting rules.\n\n45. **(severity HIGH) — Product certification scope missing MCS product certification route**  \n   **Finding:** The report discusses MCS generally and MIS 3005 installation, but does not define the product certification route needed for UK grant-eligible heat pumps.  \n   **Evidence:** “UK installer + product certification required for Boiler Upgrade Scheme grant eligibility”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 1 compliance registry — `src/registries/standards/mcs.ts` lacks product-certification standard mapping and test evidence requirements.  \n   **Fix:** Add MCS product-certification scope entries, including required test reports, declared capacity/COP/SCOP, sound power, installation manual, and factory production control evidence.\n\n46. **(severity HIGH) — Claimed BS EN 12102-1 acoustic validation is unsupported**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims octave-band validation without measurement data, test setup, declared sound power, or accredited lab path.  \n   **Evidence:** “Acoustic performance is validated to BS EN 12102-1 with octave-band measurements confirming compliance below 56 dBA sound power.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 compliance prose — `src/chain/stages/modules/complianceNarrative.ts` allows “validated” claims before test evidence exists.  \n   **Fix:** Ban words like “validated,” “certified,” and “confirmed” unless linked to evidence artifact; otherwise render “design target.”\n\n47. **(severity MED) — IP rating and acoustic lining conflict not checked**  \n   **Finding:** The design puts mineral wool and open-cell foam inside an outdoor IPX4 unit without moisture/fire/acoustic durability validation.  \n   **Evidence:** “25 mm thick mineral-wool acoustic lining on all internal surfaces” and “IPX4 splash protection rating”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 material compatibility — `src/chain/stages/materials/environmentalCompatibility.ts` does not evaluate absorbent materials in wet outdoor airflow paths.  \n   **Fix:** Add environmental compatibility checks for acoustic materials: water absorption, mould, freeze/thaw, flame class, airflow erosion, and condensate exposure.\n\n48. **(severity HIGH) — BoM includes fake/manufacturer-mismatched parts but still prices them**  \n   **Finding:** The report identifies manufacturer/part mismatches, e.g. Vishay thermistor listed as TE Connectivity, but keeps them in BoM.  \n   **Evidence:** “The part number NTCLE100E3103JB0 is manufactured by Vishay / BC Components, not TE Connectivity as claimed by the user.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4.5 part verifier — `src/chain/stages/procurement/verifyPartNumbers.ts` does not overwrite canonical manufacturer/MPN after resolving mismatch.  \n   **Fix:** If verifier finds a manufacturer mismatch, update BoM to verified manufacturer/MPN or mark line invalid; never keep the hallucinated pair.\n\n49. **(severity MED) — Appendix recommendation repeats wrong pressure sensor for two different ranges**  \n   **Finding:** The same recommended Danfoss part number is given for both 0–50 bar and 0–15 bar sensors, which cannot both be correct if ranges differ.  \n   **Evidence:** “Recommended part number 060G1122” under both “high pressure sensor” and “low pressure sensor”  \n   **Root cause:** Supplier recommendation fallback — `src/chain/stages/procurement/recommendReplacement.ts` reuses nearest successful SKU without validating parametric attributes.  \n   **Fix:** Replacement recommender must re-run parametric checks for pressure range, output, port, wetted material, voltage, and temperature before accepting a SKU.\n\n50. **(severity HIGH) — Appendix recommends R410A compressor as R290-compatible**  \n   **Finding:** The replacement recommendation falsely says the ZP25K5E-TF5 is widely used in R290-compatible applications, contradicting the physics critic.  \n   **Evidence:** “This model is widely used in R290-compatible heat pump and refrigeration applications” and “The Copeland ZP25K5E-TFM is an R410A scroll compressor, not rated or safe for R290”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 4.5 replacement recommender and physics critic are not reconciled — `src/chain/stages/procurement/recommendReplacement.ts` runs independently of `physicsCritic.ts`.  \n   **Fix:** Add cross-stage contradiction resolver: safety-critical recommender outputs must be checked against physics critic and refrigerant-compatibility rules before rendering.\n\n51. **(severity HIGH) — Report claims no TBD while Appendix says manual sourcing required**  \n   **Finding:** The cover and BoM summary say no TBD items, while Appendix A repeatedly requires manual sourcing.  \n   **Evidence:** “0 TBD” and “MANUAL SOURCING REQUIRED”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 6 renderer — `src/report/renderCover.ts` counts only price-null rows as TBD and ignores `manual_sourcing_required`.  \n   **Fix:** Define TBD as `price_missing OR verification_failed OR manual_sourcing_required OR fabricated_sku`; update cover metrics accordingly.\n\n52. **(severity MED) — Class/module names leak generic taxonomy instead of product terminology**  \n   **Finding:** The BoM uses abstract class names like “Mass Fluid Transport Process” instead of the user-facing module names, hurting traceability.  \n   **Evidence:** “Module 3 — Mass Fluid Transport Process” while earlier module is “R290 Refrigerant & Hydronic Piping”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 6 BoM renderer — `src/report/renderBom.ts` uses internal ontology labels instead of final module display names.  \n   **Fix:** Store both `ontology_class` and `display_name`; render display names and keep ontology only in metadata/debug.\n\n53. **(severity MED) — Standards table jurisdiction classification is incoherent**  \n   **Finding:** BS EN 378-1 and UK electrical regulations are marked as “industry” while EU directives are marked EU, even for a UK-target product.  \n   **Evidence:** “BS EN 378-1 … industry Mandatory” and “UK Electrical Equipment Safety Regulations industry Mandatory”  \n   **Root cause:** Compliance registry schema — `src/registries/standards/schema.ts` lacks clear distinction among jurisdiction, legal force, harmonised/designated standard, and de-facto market requirement.  \n   **Fix:** Refactor fields to `jurisdiction`, `legal_instrument`, `designated_standard`, `scheme_requirement`, `market_requirement`.\n\n54. **(severity MED) — OTA updates via Modbus are unrealistic / underspecified**  \n   **Finding:** The report claims OTA firmware updates through a Modbus gateway without defining network path, bootloader, security, or cybersecurity compliance.  \n   **Evidence:** “Firmware supports OTA updates via the Modbus gateway.”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 controls narrative — `src/chain/stages/modules/controlNarrative.ts` injects generic IoT features without architecture support.  \n   **Fix:** Require communications architecture before OTA claims: physical link, gateway, authentication, secure boot, rollback, update transport, and UK/EU cybersecurity obligations.\n\n55. **(severity MED) — Controller power design incomplete**  \n   **Finding:** The controller uses a 24 V bus and a 5 V DC/DC converter but no specified 230 VAC to 24 V PSU.  \n   **Evidence:** “stepping down the 24 V DC control bus to a stable 5 V rail”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 electrical architecture — `src/chain/stages/electrical/powerTree.ts` does not enforce complete power-tree closure from mains to every rail.  \n   **Fix:** Add power-tree solver: every load rail must have upstream source, conversion efficiency, protection, isolation, and current budget.\n\n56. **(severity MED) — HMI cost is physically impossible**  \n   **Finding:** A 5-inch LCD touchscreen HMI panel is priced at £0.14.  \n   **Evidence:** “Touchscreen HMI Panel Custom OEM design HP-HMI-PNL-A1 ~ £0.14”  \n   **Root cause:** Engine B class mapping — `src/chain/stages/bom/classifyPart.ts` classifies HMI as low-cost “optical” rather than display assembly.  \n   **Fix:** Add component class `display_hmi_assembly` with minimum costs by size/touch/interface and require quote or credible module reference.\n\n57. **(severity MED) — PCB pricing is impossible**  \n   **Finding:** A conformal-coated 4-layer inverter PCB with heatsink is priced at £1.14.  \n   **Evidence:** “HP-INV-PCB-A1 … FR4 4-layer 1.6 mm with conformal coating, 200x150x25 mm including heatsink … ~ £1.14”  \n   **Root cause:** Engine B pricing — `src/chain/stages/bom/classCostCurves.ts` applies bare-board commodity PCB pricing to assembled power electronics.  \n   **Fix:** Split `bare_pcb`, `pcba`, `power_stage`, `heatsink`, `conformal_coating`, `test`; never price “PCB including heatsink” as bare PCB.\n\n58. **(severity HIGH) — Heat-exchanger pricing impossible**  \n   **Finding:** Both the brazed plate condenser and finned evaporator are priced at £2.96, which is not credible for 8 kW pressure-rated heat exchangers.  \n   **Evidence:** “Brazed Plate Condenser SWEP (Dover) B10T-30 ~ £2.96” and “Finned Tube Evaporator Kelvion (custom OEM) EVAP-8K-R290-A1 ~ £2.96”  \n   **Root cause:** Engine B fallback pricing — `src/chain/stages/bom/priceFallback.ts` uses small thermal-component priors for major pressure assemblies.  \n   **Fix:** Add major-assembly price floors and require supplier quote for pressure-rated heat exchangers above 1 kW.\n\n59. **(severity HIGH) — Compressor acoustic claim depends on fake/custom jacket and fake compressor geometry**  \n   **Finding:** The acoustic strategy is built around a compressor jacket for an invalid compressor SKU.  \n   **Evidence:** “Custom moulded for ZP25K5E” and “ZP25K5E-TFM is an R410A scroll compressor”  \n   **Root cause:** Stage 2 acoustic design — `src/chain/stages/modules/acousticDesign.ts` binds acoustic parts to unverified compressor before procurement verification.  \n   **Fix:** Defer geometry-dependent acoustic parts until primary compressor SKU is verified; add dependency invalidation when parent part changes.\n\n60. **(severity HIGH) — Report is not procurement-ready but still presents a purchasable BoM**  \n   **Finding:** The document has multiple manual-review gates fired yet still produces a detailed priced BoM and supplier list.  \n   **Evidence:** “MANUAL REVIEW — G1b compliance”, “MANUAL REVIEW — G3 completeness”, “MANUAL REVIEW — G5 part numbers”, and “MANUAL REVIEW — Physics critic”  \n   **Root cause:** Pipeline orchestration — `src/chain/runPipeline.ts` treats exhausted gates as annotations instead of output-mode changes.  \n   **Fix:** If any safety/compliance/procurement gate exhausts retries, downgrade report mode to “concept only,” suppress precise cost totals, and watermark BoM “not for procurement.”","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating electrical components**\n\nIt seems that the 20uF capacitor connected to the DC link isn't sufficient for my needs. I'm also noticing that the 6A EMI filter isn't cutting it either. Additionally, the 10uF capacitors rated at 450V are showing signs of being under-dimensioned for the ripple on the DC link. I really nee